.TH RAILWAY-VARIABLE-AUDITOR-CSV 5 "October 2026" "Railway Variable Auditor 0.1.0" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
railway-variable-auditor-csv \- files written by the Railway variable audit
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR railway-variable-auditor (1)
and
.BR railway-variable-audit (1)
write their files into a run folder named
.IR run-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS ,
in UTC, readable only by its owner. The CSV files are UTF-8 with a header row and RFC 4180 quoting (fields containing a comma, a quote or a line break are wrapped in double quotes, and a quote inside is doubled). Timestamps use ISO 8601 in UTC, for example
.BR 2026-10-22T08:30:00Z .
.PP
Only
.I audit.csv
holds values.
.PP
The audit writes one file,
.IR audit.csv ,
and reads
.IR policy.ini .
The other files here (changes.csv, roles.csv, not-changed.csv, deploys.csv, progress.csv, undo-report.csv and undo-preview.csv) belong to the rotation code that was set aside. No program writes them now. They are described so that files from an earlier build can still be read.
.SH FILES
.SS audit.csv
Written by
.BR railway-variable-auditor (1)
and
.BR railway-variable-audit (1).
One row for each variable of each service and environment, and for each shared variable.
.B Contains live secrets.
Owner only. Delete it when you have finished.
.TP
.B workspace, project, environment, service, variable
As in
.IR changes.csv .
The service is \fBshared\fR for an environment level shared variable.
.TP
.B value
The stored value. A reference appears as written. A sealed variable appears empty.
.TP
.B category, reason
What the classifier thinks (see below), to help with the work. Nothing is changed.
.TP
.B (categories)
The values of the category column are listed under
.I not-changed.csv
below.
.SS changes.csv (set aside)
One row for each variable in each container that will be changed. Written, and flushed to disk, before anything is sent to Railway.
.TP
.B workspace, project, environment
Names.
.TP
.B service
The service name, or
.B shared
for a variable at environment level.
.TP
.B variable
Variable name.
.TP
.B old_value, new_value
The value before and after the change.
.TP
.B timestamp
When the file was written, before anything was sent. When each step was accepted is in
.IR progress.csv .
.TP
.B workspace_id, project_id, environment_id, service_id
Railway ids. Staging and undo use these, so a rename does not break them.
.B service_id
is empty for an environment level shared variable.
.PP
.B This file contains live secrets.
It is created readable only by its owner (mode 0600 on Linux and macOS, a restricted access list on Windows, which is not yet tested). Keep it until you have checked your services, because undo needs it, then delete it.
.SS roles.csv (set aside)
One row for each database role that will have its password changed. Only written when there are roles. Written before anything is sent.
.TP
.B project_id, environment_id, db_service_id, project, environment, db_service
Which Postgres service.
.TP
.B role, database, host, port
The role, the database in its connection URL, and the host and port from the URL (for the report: the connection itself goes through the secure tunnel).
.TP
.B old_password, new_password
The passwords before and after.
.PP
.B This file contains live secrets.
It is owner only, like
.IR changes.csv .
Undo needs it. Keep it until you have checked your services, then delete it.
.SS not-changed.csv (set aside, but its categories are used by audit.csv)
One row for each variable that was deliberately left alone. No values.
.TP
.B workspace, project, environment, service, variable
As in
.IR changes.csv .
.TP
.B category
One of the following. The reason column explains the choice.
.RS
.TP
.B ini-config
INI file content, which may hold tokens for other systems such as Xero. Change by hand.
.TP
.B database-credential
A credential for a Railway database, or a pasted copy of one, that is not a rotatable role. Use Regenerate on the database service for the template's own login, and replace pasted copies with references. For a role that could not be rotated safely the reason says why (for example, the password also appears in another environment).
.TP
.B database-role
The variable holds the password of an application role of a Postgres service, found through a connection URL on the private network. Change it in the database and in every variable that holds it.
.TP
.B internal-hostname
Names another container on the private network.
.TP
.B reference
The whole value is one ${{...}} reference. The source variable owns it.
.TP
.B unshared
Used in one place only, or an empty or unreadable value (it may be sealed). Most likely an API key. Rotate it with its provider.
.TP
.B shared-config
Shared, but does not look like a secret.
.TP
.B shared-manual
A shared secret the program cannot safely regenerate, or a group that you switched off on the review screen. Rotate it by hand.
.TP
.B shared
A shared secret with an internal secret name and a format that can be reproduced. Make one new value and set it everywhere it is used.
.RE
.TP
.B reason
A short plain explanation, for example
.IR "used by one service only" .
.PP
Treat every row except
.B shared-config
and
.B reference
as part of your checklist of things to rotate or check by hand. Railway's incident may have exposed these too, and this program cannot rotate a key that belongs to another company. See the website page "Checklist".
.SS run-report.csv (set aside)
One row for each workspace and project.
.TP
.B workspace, project
Names. The project is empty on a workspace row.
.TP
.B status
.BR done ,
.B skipped
or
.BR failed .
.TP
.B detail
The reason for a skipped or failed row, for example
.IR "insufficient role"
or
.IR "excluded by policy" .
.PP
Any row that is not
.B done
makes the scan incomplete.
.SS deploys.csv (set aside)
The services to redeploy: a service that has a changed variable, and any service that uses a changed variable by reference.
.TP
.B project_id, environment_id, service_id, project, environment, service
Ids and names.
.SS progress.csv (set aside)
No values. One row each time a step is accepted or fails. Staging and deploying read this file, which is what makes resuming safe.
.TP
.B phase
.BR stage ,
.BR role ,
.B deploy
or
.BR undo-deploy .
The key of a
.B role
step is the project id, environment id, database service id and role name.
.TP
.B key
The step: project id, environment id and service id (or
.BR shared ),
separated by
.BR | .
.TP
.B status, detail, timestamp
.B done
or
.BR failed ,
the reason, and when.
.SS undo-report.csv, undo-preview.csv (set aside)
Written by an undo (or by a preview, which changes nothing), one row for each row of
.IR changes.csv .
No values.
.TP
.B workspace, project, environment, service, variable
As in
.IR changes.csv .
A database role is a row with
.B role:<name>
as the variable, the database service as the service, and no workspace.
.TP
.B status
.B restored
(the old value, or the old role password, was written back),
.B conflict
(the current value is neither the new nor the old value, so it was left alone),
.B already-original
(the variable already holds the old value),
.B skipped
(the project was not selected) or
.BR failed .
.TP
.B detail, timestamp
The reason, and when.
.SS policy.ini
In the user configuration folder, for example
.IR ~/.config/railway-variable-auditor/policy.ini .
A key file with one group,
.BR [policy] ,
and these lists, separated by semicolons:
.TP
.B skip_workspaces
Workspace ids that are not scanned. A skipped workspace makes the scan incomplete.
.TP
.B never_rotate
Variables that must never be rotated, as
.IR project/environment/service/NAME .
If one member of a shared group is listed, the whole group is left alone.
.TP
.B credential_names
Extra variable names to treat as database credentials.
.TP
.B internal_names
Extra variable names that mark an internal secret.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
workspace,project,environment,service,variable,old_value,new_value,timestamp,workspace_id,project_id,environment_id,service_id
Work,alpha,production,api,SESSION_SECRET,9f2c...,41ab...,2026-10-22T08:30:00Z,w1,p1,e1,s1
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR railway-variable-auditor (1),
.BR railway-variable-audit (1),
.BR rvcore (3)
.SH AUTHORS
Ross Cameron <ross@wexfordhouse.co.za>, Wexford House.
